{"date": "2026-10-27", "post_id": 1504085, "title": "Noctilucent Clouds over Sweden", "permalink": "https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-october-27-noctilucent-clouds-over-sweden/", "media_type": "image", "explanation": "<strong>Explanation:\u00a0</strong>Sometimes it's night on the ground but day in the air. As the Earth rotates to eclipse the Sun,\u00a0<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2017-june-1-shadowrise-and-sunset/\">sunset rises up</a>from the ground. Therefore, at sunset on the ground, sunlight still shines on\u00a0<a href=\"https://gpm.nasa.gov/resources/faq/what-are-clouds-made-are-they-more-likely-form-polluted-air-or-pristine-air\">clouds</a>\u00a0above. Under\u00a0<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-october-20-an-anticrepuscular-rainbow-fan/\">usual circumstances</a>, a pretty sunset might be visible, but unusual\u00a0<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/earth/earth-observatory/noctilucent-clouds-8366/\">noctilucent clouds</a>\u00a0float so high up they can be seen well after dark. Normally too dim to be seen,\u00a0<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2024-july-9-noctilucent-clouds-over-florida/\">they</a>\u00a0may become visible just after sunset during the summer when illuminated by sunlight from below the horizon.\u00a0<a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Noctilucent_cloud\">Noctilucent cloud</a>s are the highest clouds and are known more technically as\u00a0<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/earth/earth-observatory/polar-mesospheric-clouds-7953/\">polar mesospheric clouds</a>. Featured here as they appeared in early August, a network of blue noctilucent clouds was captured in the distant sky above a field of green grass near\u00a0<a href=\"https://youtu.be/WFn7KAKZ-Wk\">Link\u00f6ping</a>,\u00a0<a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sweden\">Sweden</a>, with trees in\u00a0<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2021-october-10-full-moon-silhouettes/\">stark silhouette</a>\u00a0across the horizon. Much about\u00a0<a href=\"https://www.rmg.co.uk/stories/space-astronomy/noctilucent-clouds\">noctilucent clouds</a>\u00a0has been\u00a0<a href=\"https://science.gsfc.nasa.gov/sci/projects/14/\">discovered</a>\u00a0only in the past two decades, while\u00a0<a href=\"https://ui.adsabs.harvard.edu/abs/1997JGR...102.2001C/abstract\">how they form and evolve</a>\u00a0remains a topic of\u00a0<a href=\"https://media.istockphoto.com/id/1997900524/photo/cute-curious-cat-with-question-marks.jpg\">active research</a>.<br><br><strong>Tomorrow's picture:\u00a0</strong>round and round", "credit": "<a href=\"mailto:vojanhofer @at@ gmail .dot. com\">V. H\u00f6fer</a>, D. Ve\u010dern\u00edk &amp; B. Ve\u010dern\u00edkov\u00e1", "copyright": "<a href=\"mailto:vojanhofer @at@ gmail .dot. com\">V. H\u00f6fer</a>, D. Ve\u010dern\u00edk &amp; B. Ve\u010dern\u00edkov\u00e1", "alt": "A dark night sky is pictured over a green field bounded by trees in silhouette. Across the image middle are blue filamentary glows -- noctilucent clouds. Dark clouds are seen across the top of the image.  Please see the explanation for more detailed information.", "url": "https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-october-27-noctilucent-clouds-over-sweden/", "hdurl": "https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/october/NocClouds_Hofer_6048.jpg?w=6048&h=4030&fit=clip&crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint", "basic_html": "<!doctype html>\n<html>\n<head>\n<meta charset=\"UTF-8\">\n<meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1\">\n<title> APOD: 2026 October 27 - Noctilucent Clouds over Sweden</title>\n<link rel=\"canonical\" href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-october-27-noctilucent-clouds-over-sweden/\">\n<!-- gsfc meta tags -->\n<meta name=\"orgcode\" content=\"661\">\n<meta name=\"rno\" content=\"Dana Bolles\">\n<meta name=\"content-owner\" content=\"Robert Nemiroff\">\n<meta name=\"webmaster\" content=\"Stephen.F.Fantasia.1\">\n<meta name=\"description\" content=\"Explanation:\u00a0Sometimes it&#039;s night on the ground but day in the air. As the Earth rotates to eclipse the Sun,\u00a0sunset rises upfrom the ground. Therefore, at sunset on the ground, sunlight still shines on\u00a0clouds\u00a0above. Under\u00a0usual circumstances, a pretty sunset might be visible, but unusual\u00a0noctilucent clouds\u00a0float so high up they can be seen well after dark. Normally too dim to be seen,\u00a0they\u00a0may become visible just after sunset during the summer when illuminated by sunlight from below the horizon.\u00a0Noctilucent clouds are the highest clouds and are known more technically as\u00a0polar mesospheric clouds. Featured here as they appeared in early August, a network of blue noctilucent clouds was captured in the distant sky above a field of green grass near\u00a0Link\u00f6ping,\u00a0Sweden, with trees in\u00a0stark silhouette\u00a0across the horizon. Much about\u00a0noctilucent clouds\u00a0has been\u00a0discovered\u00a0only in the past two decades, while\u00a0how they form and evolve\u00a0remains a topic of\u00a0active research.Tomorrow&#039;s picture:\u00a0round and round\">\n<!-- -->\n<meta name=\"keywords\" content=\"noctilucent clouds over sweden\">\n<meta property=\"og:type\" content=\"article\">\n<meta property=\"og:title\" content=\"Noctilucent Clouds over Sweden\">\n<meta property=\"og:url\" content=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-october-27-noctilucent-clouds-over-sweden/\">\n<meta property=\"og:site_name\" content=\"NASA Science\">\n<meta property=\"og:image\" content=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/october/NocClouds_Hofer_6048.jpg?w=6048&#038;h=4030&#038;fit=clip&#038;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint\">\n<meta property=\"og:image:secure_url\" content=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/october/NocClouds_Hofer_6048.jpg?w=6048&#038;h=4030&#038;fit=clip&#038;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint\">\n<meta property=\"og:image:alt\" content=\"A dark night sky is pictured over a green field bounded by trees in silhouette. Across the image middle are blue filamentary glows -- noctilucent clouds. Dark clouds are seen across the top of the image.  Please see the explanation for more detailed information.\">\n<meta property=\"article:published_time\" content=\"2026-10-27T00:05:00-04:00\">\n<meta property=\"article:modified_time\" content=\"2026-10-26T11:13:49-04:00\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:card\" content=\"summary_large_image\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:title\" content=\"Noctilucent Clouds over Sweden\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:site\" content=\"@NASA\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:creator\" content=\"@NASA\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:image\" content=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/october/NocClouds_Hofer_6048.jpg?w=6048&#038;h=4030&#038;fit=clip&#038;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint\">\n</head>\n<body BGCOLOR=\"#F4F4FF\" text=\"#000000\" link=\"#0000FF\" vlink=\"#7F0F9F\"\nalink=\"#FF0000\">\n\n<center>\n<h1> Astronomy Picture of the Day </h1>\n<p>\n\n<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Discover the cosmos!</a>\nEach day a different image or photograph of our fascinating universe is\nfeatured, along with a brief explanation written by a professional astronomer.\n<p>\n\n2026 October 27<br>\n\n<a href=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/october/NocClouds_Hofer_6048.jpg\">\n<IMG SRC=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/october/NocClouds_Hofer_6048.jpg\" alt=\"A dark night sky is pictured over a green field bounded by trees in silhouette. Across the image middle are blue filamentary glows -- noctilucent clouds. Dark clouds are seen across the top of the image.  Please see the explanation for more detailed information.\"></a>\n</center>\n\n<center>\n<b> Noctilucent Clouds over Sweden </b> <br>\n\n<b>Image Credit &\n<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/about-apod/\">Copyright</a>:</b>\n\n<a href=\"mailto:vojanhofer @at@ gmail .dot. com\">V. H\u00f6fer</a>, D. Ve\u010dern\u00edk &amp; B. Ve\u010dern\u00edkov\u00e1\n</center>\n\n<p>\n\n<b> Explanation: </b>\n\n<strong>Explanation:\u00a0</strong>Sometimes it's night on the ground but day in the air. As the Earth rotates to eclipse the Sun,\u00a0<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2017-june-1-shadowrise-and-sunset/\">sunset rises up</a>from the ground. Therefore, at sunset on the ground, sunlight still shines on\u00a0<a href=\"https://gpm.nasa.gov/resources/faq/what-are-clouds-made-are-they-more-likely-form-polluted-air-or-pristine-air\">clouds</a>\u00a0above. Under\u00a0<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-october-20-an-anticrepuscular-rainbow-fan/\">usual circumstances</a>, a pretty sunset might be visible, but unusual\u00a0<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/earth/earth-observatory/noctilucent-clouds-8366/\">noctilucent clouds</a>\u00a0float so high up they can be seen well after dark. Normally too dim to be seen,\u00a0<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2024-july-9-noctilucent-clouds-over-florida/\">they</a>\u00a0may become visible just after sunset during the summer when illuminated by sunlight from below the horizon.\u00a0<a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Noctilucent_cloud\">Noctilucent cloud</a>s are the highest clouds and are known more technically as\u00a0<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/earth/earth-observatory/polar-mesospheric-clouds-7953/\">polar mesospheric clouds</a>. Featured here as they appeared in early August, a network of blue noctilucent clouds was captured in the distant sky above a field of green grass near\u00a0<a href=\"https://youtu.be/WFn7KAKZ-Wk\">Link\u00f6ping</a>,\u00a0<a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sweden\">Sweden</a>, with trees in\u00a0<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2021-october-10-full-moon-silhouettes/\">stark silhouette</a>\u00a0across the horizon. Much about\u00a0<a href=\"https://www.rmg.co.uk/stories/space-astronomy/noctilucent-clouds\">noctilucent clouds</a>\u00a0has been\u00a0<a href=\"https://science.gsfc.nasa.gov/sci/projects/14/\">discovered</a>\u00a0only in the past two decades, while\u00a0<a href=\"https://ui.adsabs.harvard.edu/abs/1997JGR...102.2001C/abstract\">how they form and evolve</a>\u00a0remains a topic of\u00a0<a href=\"https://media.istockphoto.com/id/1997900524/photo/cute-curious-cat-with-question-marks.jpg\">active research</a>.<br><br><strong>Tomorrow's picture:\u00a0</strong>round and round\n<p> <center>\n\n\n<p> <hr>\n<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod/2026-october-26-m42-the-great-nebula-in-orion/\">&lt;</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Archive</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Index</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Search</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/calendar/\">Calendar</a>\n| <a href=\"/feed/apod-basic/\">RSS</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/education-links/\">Education</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/about-apod/\">About APOD</a>\n| &gt;\n\n<hr><p>\n<b> Authors & editors: </b>\nRobert Nemiroff, Jerry Bonnell, Cecilia Chirenti, Keighley Rockcliffe<br>\n<b>NASA Official: </b> Dana Bolles<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/about-apod/\">Specific rights apply</a>.<br>\n<a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/about/highlights/HP_Privacy.html\">NASA Web\nPrivacy Policy and Important Notices</a><br>\n<b>A service of:</b>\n<a href=\"http://astrophysics.gsfc.nasa.gov/\">ASD</a> at\n<a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/\">NASA</a> /\n<a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/centers/goddard/\">GSFC</a>\n<br><b>&</b> <a href=\"http://www.mtu.edu/\">Michigan Tech. U.</a><br>\n</center>\n</body>\n</html>", "basic_html_url": "https://science.nasa.gov/wp-json/wp/v2/apod-basic/261027/html"}